WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.000
Whatever happened to Jabba the Hutt's son? Where'd&nbsp;
the Crimson Dawn go? Who was Snoke, anyway? Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.080
watching for more "Star Wars" storylines that were&nbsp;
lost a long time ago in a galaxy far, far away.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.920
Star Wars: The Clone Wars is often commended&nbsp;
by fans for filling in the many blanks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.480
had been left behind by the prequel trilogy.&nbsp;
But one plot point that neither the show nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.680
the films ever elaborated on was the notion&nbsp;
that Anakin had no father. Theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.400
he was conceived via the Force, similar to&nbsp;
Mary's virgin birth of Jesus Christ in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.400
Christian religion, but it's never mentioned again&nbsp;
after Shmi Skywalker first tells Qui-Gon Jinn.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.520
How this happened in the Disney canon is still&nbsp;
a mystery. In Legends, the Darth Plagueis novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.040
explained that Palpatine's master had discovered&nbsp;
how to influence the force to such a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.840
that he could use it to create life itself.&nbsp;
He taught Palpatine how to use this power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.960
and when they experimented with it, they&nbsp;
accidentally created Anakin. This revelation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.520
meant that the Sith had literally created the&nbsp;
person that would eventually destroy them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.560
and the idea had a sort of poetic justice&nbsp;
to it that many fans found quite appealing.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.800
Back when The Phantom Menace&nbsp;
was released, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.680
details about Shmi's immaculate conception had&nbsp;
been received with indifference by many — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.240
likely led the main Star Wars universe to&nbsp;
downplay it in subsequent installments.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.480
In 2008, Lucasfilm released an animated&nbsp;
movie called Star Wars: The Clone Wars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.120
This was basically a theatrical pilot for the&nbsp;
upcoming television series of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.760
and its plot revolved around Jabba the Hutt's son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.400
Rotta. Secretly kidnapped by Count Dooku's&nbsp;
agents, the party that managed to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.360
Rotta to his father would be granted access&nbsp;
to key hyperspace lanes controlled by Jabba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.720
greatly aiding their war efforts. Anakin&nbsp;
Skywalker and his new apprentice, Ahsoka Tano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.840
eventually rescue Rotta and secure Jabba's help…&nbsp;
and then Rotta the Hutt is never seen again.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.280
To this day, fans have no idea what became of&nbsp;
the little Hutt-let. Certainly, by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.600
Jabba is killed by Princess Leia and&nbsp;
her crew, Rotta is nowhere to be seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.480
It's quite possible that Rotta had simply moved&nbsp;
out of Jabba's Tatooine Palace by this point, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.360
that he's still happily operating a crime cartel&nbsp;
in some other distant corner of the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.280
But it’s a surprising fact that a character as&nbsp;
significant as Jabba has a son whose fate remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.760
unknown — especially since a continuation&nbsp;
of his story could be pretty interesting.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.280
When Count Dooku's droid army captures&nbsp;
Obi-Wan Kenobi in Attack of the Clones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.840
he tells Obi-Wan that a Sith Lord is&nbsp;
controlling the Republic's Senate.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.320
[laughter]

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.040
Yet, despite the fact that this admission should&nbsp;
have been a major red flag for the Jedi Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.640
the Jedi do nothing with this&nbsp;
knowledge. As far as fans know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.200
the Jedi never investigate the claim&nbsp;
or look into it in any meaningful way.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.800
This plot point could have been a&nbsp;
major revelation, one with serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.320
ramifications spawning numerous storylines.&nbsp;
Instead, the claim is never followed up on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.160
only mentioned a few times in subsequent Star Wars&nbsp;
iterations and dismissed half-heartedly each time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.480
Though it makes sense that the Jedi wouldn't&nbsp;
take the villain at his word, it is strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.360
that they've never investigated it, either&nbsp;
on-screen or in the pages of a comic or novel.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.760
Over the course of Episodes 18 and 19 of Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.280
The Clone Wars introduced its&nbsp;
own version of Godzilla. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.080
named the creature the "Zillo Beast" as an&nbsp;
obvious homage to the Japanese creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.960
and soon had the monster rampaging through the&nbsp;
streets of Coruscant. In the first episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.760
"The Zillo Beast", the creature is awakened from&nbsp;
its long slumber on the planet of Malastare.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.520
Though the planet's natives want&nbsp;
to destroy and kill the creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.480
Palpatine and the Jedi secretly save the&nbsp;
creature's life and bring it to Coruscant to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.200
and preserve it. The creature briefly escapes&nbsp;
during "The Zillo Beast Strikes Back," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.240
before the Republic finally manages to kill&nbsp;
it. The episode ends with Palpatine taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.880
its body for further study and ordering&nbsp;
his head scientist to attempt to clone it.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.600
It is never mentioned again. To this day, fans&nbsp;
don't know what, if anything, came about as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.200
part of this research. Of course, the in-universe&nbsp;
explanation would be that Palpatine's scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.000
failed, since Zillo clones have never been&nbsp;
seen and no lightsaber-resistant armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.800
has ever materialized within the Empire.&nbsp;
But it's not certain why the show's creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.040
chose to end the episode on something of a&nbsp;
cliffhanger, only to never address it again.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.480
When Lucasfilm released Solo: A&nbsp;
Star Wars Story in theaters in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.400
nobody could have anticipated one of its most&nbsp;
surprising cameos: Maul. Played in person once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.000
again by Ray Park, with Rebels and Clone Wars&nbsp;
actor Sam Witwer returning to voice him, Darth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.280
Maul is revealed in the latter half of the film&nbsp;
as the head of the dreaded Crimson Dawn cartel.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.080
This is another storyline that would never&nbsp;
be seen again. The next time fans see Maul in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.960
the timeline is throughout the second and third&nbsp;
seasons of Rebels, by which point Maul has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.120
stranded alone on Malachor for years and no longer&nbsp;
leads anybody, let alone a vast criminal network.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.640
Although it's still unknown exactly how Maul went&nbsp;
from intra-galactic crime boss to lone survivor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.600
one possible answer might be found in&nbsp;
an old interview with Alden Ehrenreich.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.000
According to Ehrenreich, he had signed on&nbsp;
for three films, potentially indicating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.280
that Lucasfilm had been planning on making a&nbsp;
Solo trilogy of sorts. If this was the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.160
the next two movies might have featured Maul more&nbsp;
prominently. If nothing else, though, there's at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.520
least a chance fans might get to learn how he fell&nbsp;
from grace in Marvel's Crimson Reign mini-series.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.200
The Force Awakens introduced&nbsp;
a new alien to the franchise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.520
Maz Kanata. A friend of Han&nbsp;
Solo and apparently Chewie's ex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.560
Maz is the "pirate queen" of Takodana — and&nbsp;
before her castle is destroyed by the First Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.080
it acts as a hub for smugglers from across the&nbsp;
galaxy. A force-sensitive herself, Maz spent her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.280
millennia-long lifetime collecting artifacts of&nbsp;
all kinds within the storehouses of her castle.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.080
One surprising item in her collection is&nbsp;
the lightsaber that Anakin Skywalker built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.640
during the Clone Wars, which later passed&nbsp;
down to Luke. Before The Force Awakens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.160
the blade had last been seen falling into the&nbsp;
abyss during Luke's duel with Vader on Cloud City.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:17.440
"That lightsaber was Luke's and his father's&nbsp;
before him, and now it calls to you."

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:24.240
Many understandably believed the lightsaber to be&nbsp;
lost forever, and yet Maz Kanata somehow found it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.920
But how? If Maz has been alive for a millennia,&nbsp;
where was she during the other films? Did she know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:35.440
any other Jedi, and could she even appear in the&nbsp;
ongoing High Republic project? Though the fate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.160
the weapon was teased in the pages of the comics,&nbsp;
nothing more was revealed about it other than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.400
was found. Fans can only hope that they'll&nbsp;
one day get the answers to these questions.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.080
One of the major turning points in The Force&nbsp;
Awakens is the destruction of the New Republic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.920
capital at the hands of the First Order's newest&nbsp;
super-weapon, Starkiller Base. Subsequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.320
many fans speculated about what might&nbsp;
happen to the New Republic in the next film.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.200
Founded after the events of Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;
the New Republic had been a major facet of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.760
"Legends" stories set in that time period,&nbsp;
and so speculation ran amok as to what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.880
might look like in the canon universe. Sadly,&nbsp;
the New Republic never made a proper appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.240
in the sequel trilogy. In both The Last Jedi&nbsp;
and The Rise of Skywalker, the New Republic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:26.000
simply gone. Apparently, audiences were meant to&nbsp;
believe the destruction of its capital immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.880
and totally destroyed the entire Republic&nbsp;
for the second time in the Skywalker saga.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.880
This was a disappointing choice for those who&nbsp;
wished to see the promises of the Original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.080
Trilogy fulfilled. And even though we've seen&nbsp;
glimpses of the New Republic in The Mandalorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.520
and Star Wars: Squadrons, the movies' treatment&nbsp;
of this new government leaves a lot to be desired.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.320
The Force Awakens introduced the&nbsp;
galaxy to a brand new supervillain:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.720
the mysterious Supreme Leader Snoke.&nbsp;
In the wake of the movie's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.920
fans and media outlets around the world&nbsp;
speculated wildly about Snoke's "real identity,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:03.680
putting forth all kinds of wild&nbsp;
theories about who he might be.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.960
Then The Last Jedi arrived in theaters in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.680
and not only did it provide fans with no new&nbsp;
information about the Supreme Leader, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.720
killed off the villain at the end of the second&nbsp;
act. Snoke's identity and backstory apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:22.080
didn't matter to the wider story, a sentiment&nbsp;
that was only reinforced in The Rise of Skywalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.520
in which Snoke is revealed to&nbsp;
be a creation of Darth Sidious.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.640
Though his "identity" has seemingly been answered,&nbsp;
what little clues that fans were given about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:36.160
history and backstory were tantalizing, hinting&nbsp;
that he and Luke Skywalker had already met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:40.720
at least once in the past. Snoke may have&nbsp;
been little more than a tool of Palpatine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:46.640
but his backstory did hold potential for&nbsp;
future exploration — arguably, it still does.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.840
During the events of The Rise of Skywalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.200
as the movie's heroes find themselves&nbsp;
trapped in quicksand, Finn yells this to Rey:

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.600
"Rey! Rey, I never told you-"

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.160
The entire group would miraculously&nbsp;
emerge unscathed from the encounter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.280
yet for some reason Finn never felt the need to&nbsp;
continue his train of thought. Many assumed he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.720
wanted to say "I love you," but various comments&nbsp;
from the cast have cast doubt on this theory.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.760
John Boyega, who played Finn, has since confirmed&nbsp;
that this isn't what he was supposed to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.080
but he hasn't provided an alternate answer,&nbsp;
either. Instead, fans will likely have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.280
to continue relying on rumored reports of an&nbsp;
overheard conversation at the film's premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:33.040
in which J.J. Abrams reportedly said that Finn&nbsp;
was about to tell Rey he was force sensitive.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.400
Finn is able to identify the command ship&nbsp;
correctly by 'feeling' at the Battle of Exegol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.960
In theory, he has the potential to become a&nbsp;
Jedi - an idea that fits well with another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.080
open-ended plot point that appears later in the&nbsp;
film. When the team travels to the ruins of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.760
second Death Star, they meet a battalion of First&nbsp;
Order Stormtroopers who have all chosen to rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.360
of their own accord, just like Finn. Though it's&nbsp;
not stated explicitly, it is implied that each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:04.960
of the former stormtroopers who rebelled did so&nbsp;
because they were force sensitive as well. Alas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.000
this storyline was dropped almost immediately,&nbsp;
and it seems unlikely that it will ever return.

